WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.880
Traditional steel plants cost billions to build from ore, yet Minimils using scrap and electricity

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:09.400
started far cheaper and kept moving up the product ladder.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.360
This is First Principles Daily, episode 63.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:19.640
Integrated steel works once demanded enormous capital, continuous ore supply, and Coke

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.760
ovens just to make basic rebar.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.860
Minimil operators looked at the same rebar and asked what it actually required if the input

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.220
was already steel sitting in scrap yards.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:35.240
The answer was an electric arc furnace, power, and a much smaller building.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.560
That single shift let new entrance start at the bottom and move steadily into higher

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:41.380
value steel.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:47.340
The surprise was not merely lower startup cost, but the discovery that the entire upstream

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:52.720
chain of mining, coaking, and reduction could be bypassed when the atoms were already present

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:53.780
in usable form.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:59.520
For most of the 20th century, the default steel plant began with iron ore, limestone,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.040
and metallurgical coal.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:07.160
Operators followed the pattern set by earlier blast furnace practice because the capital already

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:07.780
existed.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.900
The supply chains were locked in, and regulators and customers treated the integrated root

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.520
as the only proven method.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:21.120
A new integrated mill required Coke batteries, center plants, blast furnaces, and oxygen

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:21.920
converters.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:27.880
Plus the land, rail, and port infrastructure to move millions of tons of raw material each

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:28.240
year.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:34.200
Capital costs ran into the billions because every step had to be sized for continuous high

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.020
volume operation, from cold raw materials.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:43.820
Finished hot rolled coil or rebar therefore carried the accumulated expense of mining, transport,

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.740
coke making, reduction, and conversion.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.700
Plus the labor and maintenance overhead of that entire chain.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:56.060
Builders accepted these costs because they saw no alternative that could guarantee the

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:00.080
volume and quality demanded by construction and automotive buyers.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.360
The assumption was that steel making was inherently a massive or to finish goods enterprise,

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:10.560
so any newcomer had to replicate the same scale or stay out.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.640
That assumption kept the industry concentrated in a handful of large, long established companies.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:22.960
When demand softened, the fixed costs of those integrated complexes remained, making it

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.940
difficult to reduce output without enormous losses.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:31.820
The result was an industry whose pricing reflected the full weight of its historical infrastructure

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:36.720
rather than the minimum physical requirements of turning iron into usable shapes.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:42.560
Minimil founders began instead with the scrap already circulating in the economy.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:48.440
The magic wand floor for their product is therefore the market price of that scrap, plus

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:53.720
the electricity needed to melt it, rather than the cost of extracting and reducing virgin

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:54.060
ore.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.840
Scrap prices fluctuate with demand, but are routinely a few hundred dollars per ton.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:05.740
Electricity at typical industrial rates adds another few tens of dollars per ton melted.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:11.940
Adding those two inputs gives a raw material plus energy floor well below the capital and

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.240
operating burden of an integrated mill.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:20.020
The idiot index of the older plants was therefore high because their finished price reflected

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:25.720
decades of accumulated plant, logistics, and process overhead rather than the intrinsic

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:27.260
value of the iron atoms.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.260
The first concrete redesign was to replace the blast furnace and converter with a single

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.060
electric arc furnace that simply melts scrap.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:42.860
That move eliminated coke ovens, center strands, and ore yards cutting both capital per ton

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.440
and the number of process steps that could fail.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:51.340
The second move was to site the furnace near scrap sources and rolling mills so that the

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:55.420
liquid steel could be cast and rolled with minimal reheating and transport.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:01.300
The third move was to standardize on smaller, repeatable plant designs that could be built

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:07.740
in months rather than years, lowering financing costs and allowing incremental capacity additions.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.780
Each of these steps attacked a different layer of the old cost stack.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:17.780
The EAF removed the chemical reduction stage, the local siding removed logistics.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.460
The modular construction removed the scale penalty.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:27.180
The key engineering trade-off was accepting that scrap chemistry varies, which required

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:32.640
better sorting, real-time spectroscopy, and downstream ladle metallurgy to hold final

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.540
composition within specification.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:40.540
Winning that trade-off, let the plants move from rebar into higher grades without replicating

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:42.840
the entire ore-based infrastructure.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:48.940
One further refinement was the adoption of thin slab casting directly linked to the rolling

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:54.520
mill, which eliminated the need for a separate reheating furnace between casting and rolling,

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:00.180
and thereby removed another energy intensive step that had been accepted as inevitable

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:01.720
in conventional practice.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:07.740
Another adjustment came in the form of automated scrap charging and blending systems that could

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:13.300
predict and correct for trace element carryover before the melt even began, reducing the

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:18.880
frequency of off-spec heats that would otherwise have required expensive rework or downgrading.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.520
These incremental controls did not change the raw material floor, but they narrowed the gap

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:29.520
between that floor and the delivered cost by shrinking the number of rejected tons and the

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.140
associated handling losses.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:37.180
The cumulative effect was that a minimill could reach commercial production with far less capital

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:42.420
at risk and with a cost structure that scaled more gracefully when demand rows or fell.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:48.780
Minimills now produce well over half of US steel output and have expanded from rebar into

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:55.820
bar, wire rod, and flat rolled sheet. Capital cost per annual ton of capacity sits at a fraction

00:05:55.820 --> 00:06:02.820
of the integrated root, and operating labor per ton is correspondingly lower. The new idiot index

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:09.140
is lower because the finished price is now much closer to scrap plus power, yet it remains above

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:15.220
the pure material floor because electricity prices scrap quality control and the need for occasional

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:22.700
or based iron units still add cost. Scrap supply is ultimately bounded by the existing steel stock and

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:29.340
collection rates, so very high purity or very large volume applications continue to require blast furnace

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:36.340
iron. Electricity price and carbon intensity also vary by region, limiting how far the model travels

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:43.020
without cheap, low carbon power. Even where scrap is abundant, certain alloy and specialty grades

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:48.840
still demand precise control of residual elements that current sorting and dilution practices

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:55.300
cannot always achieve at scale, preserving a residual role for ore based roots in those niches.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:01.520
A plant whose largest expense used to be the infrastructure required to reduce ore is now revealing

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:07.640
that the real variable cost is simply the price of electrons and the quality of the scrap pile already

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:14.660
in circulation. The second principle is that once the floor is set by scrap and power, every additional

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:20.300
process step must justify itself against that floor rather than against historical precedent.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:27.380
The same logic that let minimils climb from rebar into sheet now points toward the next question,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:33.880
which other materials industries are still sized for virgin feedstock when abundant scrap streams

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:40.480
already exist. That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown. The ideas travel further when

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:46.260
you pass them on. Send this to a fellow builder, I'm Patrick in Vancouver, see you tomorrow.

